@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s

@title Weaver

@s From Prepared Meaning to HTML

Woven Document prepared the `Distance` example as one numbered section with
a title, a paragraph, an equation, a named-block reference, and its code
definition. The HTML backend now chooses browser notation for those facts. Its
central result has this shape:

```html
<section class="section" id="1:1">
  <h2>1. Squared Distance.</h2>
  <p>
    The formula
    <span class="litweb-math litweb-math-inline">d^2 = x^2 + y^2</span>
    gives the squared distance.
    The implementation is in <a href="#1:1">⟨distance.rs 1⟩</a>.
  </p>
  <pre><code>fn squared_distance(x: f64, y: f64) -&gt; f64 { ... }</code></pre>
</section>
```

The real output keeps the fuller accessible named-block notation and CSS
classes explained later, but the shared facts are already visible here. The
presentation number became both a heading and an anchor. The block reference
became a link to that anchor. The equation source became a marked fallback
that Presentation can later typeset with local KaTeX. Authored text and code
are escaped for HTML rather than allowed to become markup.

This chapter follows the HTML renderer from those prepared facts to one safe
linked page. The richer `Greeting` document from Woven Document supplies the
later addition, replacement, use, and relationship examples. Book Weaving
then divides a book among several HTML pages, and Presentation surrounds those
page bodies with offline styles, equations, and syntax highlighting.

@s Three HTML Chapters, One Module

Weaver, Book Weaving, and Presentation generate one Rust module because they
cooperate on HTML pages, navigation, and browser assets. The module root keeps
their declarations in compiler order; the woven explanation introduces each
definition where its HTML job becomes useful.

--- src/weaver.rs
//! Deterministic HTML generation from parsed `.lit` programs.

@{Weaver imports}
@{Weaver public results}
